+//! Who is allowed to vote, and what it takes to change that.
+//!
+//! `meshkv/5.5-membership` is the step where the cluster stops being a fixed list. A node joins, a
+//! node leaves, and the one thing that must not happen is a committed write falling out of the
+//! quorum while the change is in flight.
+//!
+//! The answer this step implements is Raft's **joint consensus**, and it is two configurations, not
+//! one:
+//!
+//! 1. the leader appends a configuration entry that names both the **old** voters and the **new**
+//!    ones. From that moment on a decision needs a majority of *both* sets — written
+//!    [`Membership::Joint`];
+//! 2. when that entry is committed, every node adopts the new configuration and forgets the old
+//!    one — [`Membership::Stable`].
+//!
+//! Why two phases rather than one: a single switch has a window in which the old voters and the new
+//! voters each think they are the cluster, and two majorities that do not overlap can decide two
+//! different things at the same index. Requiring both majorities while the change is in flight is
+//! what makes the window safe: any majority of the old set and any majority of the new set
+//! intersect, so there is exactly one quorum at every instant.
+//!
+//! A configuration is a list of names, written `n1,n2,n3` — the same alphabet `5.1` gave a node's
+//! identity, parsed by the same type. It is carried in a log entry like any other write, which is
+//! what makes the change ordered with respect to the writes it must not lose.
+
+use crate::cluster::NodeId;
+use crate::error::Result;
+
+/// The voters of one configuration.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
